{"text":"{
  "status": "NEEDS_IMPROVEMENT",
  "core_findings": [
    "Electronics has the weakest sampled training-pair separation: negatives outrank positives in 15.53% of pairs, versus 5.25% for Industrial_and_Scientific and below 0.35% for Movies_and_TV and CDs_and_Vinyl.",
    "All four datasets show zero sampled causal-prefix violations, compatibility-view mismatches and nonfinite observations. Embedding cosine scores alone do not establish collapse."
  ],
  "key_implications": [
    "Training-pair ranking is uneven across datasets; these measurements do not establish validation HR@10.",
    "The sampled checks support numerical stability and compatibility-view consistency, but do not verify the independently generated predictor.",
    "Large Movies_and_TV and CDs_and_Vinyl margins indicate strong separation under the training sampler, without proving generalization."
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "headline_metrics": {
      "Electronics:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": 0.1553,
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": -0.4375,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": 0.0525,
      "Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_margin_mean": 11.0599,
      "CDs_and_Vinyl:pos_neg_margin_mean": 10.3956
    },
    "brief_metric_read": {
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": "The lower margin tail favors sampled negatives.",
      "causal_prefix_violation_rate": "Zero across all four datasets in sampled comparisons.",
      "view_score_mismatch_rate": "Zero across all four datasets for supplied item pairs.",
      "embedding_collapse_score": "0.6397–0.6945; positive mean cosine alignment, not proof of collapse."
    }
  }
}"}
